ly one connected component and removes it, so the next `\"1\"` encountered is guaranteed to start a different island. Each cell is pushed at most once.\n\n**Common mistakes:** marking a cell visited when popping instead of when pushing (correct but can blow up memory); counting diagonal neighbours; recursive DFS on a 300x300 all-land grid overflows Python's default recursion limit, so prefer an explicit stack or raise the limit deliberately.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** The grid is read-only? (separate visited matrix, O(mn) extra space.) The grid is huge and streams row by row? (union-find over the current and previous row, or a row-based component labelling.) Count islands as cells are added one by one (Number of Islands II)? (union-find with incremental merges, near O(1) per operation.) Return the island sizes or the largest one? (Max Area of Island: count cells during the fill.)","complexity":{"time":"O(m * n)","space":"O(m * n) worst-case stack"},"similar":["max-area-of-island","surrounded-regions","number-of-connected-components-in-an-undirected-graph"],"number":107,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def numIslands(self, grid: List[List[str]]) -> int:\n        pass\n"}